Feature: Physical presets sound like the records they were written for
  The physical-guitar presets of the seventh round were each written for a kind of record. A
  corpus of the user's own records (197 tracks in 29 groups) was separated with htdemucs_6s and
  its guitar stems measured; tests/tone_reference.hpp holds the numbers (no audio), generated by
  tools/tone_gate_profiles.py. tests/tone_features.hpp measures exactly as the corpus analysis did,
  checked against it on fourteen stems with tools/tone_measure.cpp (agreement to rounding).

  Groups whose separation the corpus analysis found unreliable (the Olivia Tremor Control's
  records, the Beatles') are not gated, and neither are presets for kinds of record the corpus
  has none of (jazz archtop, classical, the singing-sustain lead).

  Executable: tests/tone_gate_tests.cpp (CTest tone_gate_tests).

  # each_records_preset_sounds_like_the_records
  Scenario: Each records preset sounds like the records
    Given each physical preset written for a group of records, playing sixteen seconds in that style at 120 BPM
    Then its centroid, 85% roll-off, share above 3 kHz, spectral flatness, 100 ms crest and L/R correlation each land within the group's quartiles widened by 0.75 IQR
    And its third-octave shape from 100 Hz to 8 kHz lies within 4 dB on average of the group's median shape
